WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.140
The first film showed birds and bark sorting inherited moth colours.

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.420
Can people shift a trait by choosing who reproduces?

3
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.360
Keep the parent row, offspring row and changing trait frequency.

4
00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.460
The population picture still works.

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.100
Replace the moths with a row of plants that vary in an inherited trait.

6
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:27.340
Some show the wanted form strongly.

7
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.740
Others show it less.

8
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.940
Their colours match the first film's map.

9
00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.840
Each plant keeps its inherited version of the trait.

10
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:42.553
A farmer marks the plants with the wanted trait and saves seeds from those plants.

11
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:44.993
The choice is deliberate.

12
00:00:46.293 --> 00:00:48.653
The choice changes who contributes offspring.

13
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:53.513
Plants without the mark may grow, but their seeds are not used here.

14
00:00:54.483 --> 00:00:59.363
If the farmer repeats that choice, what should happen to the wanted inherited trait

15
00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:04.403
over generations? Watch the empty offspring rows before they fill.

16
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:13.407
The next row has a larger share of the wanted form.

17
00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.447
The selected form came from the starting variation.

18
00:01:18.707 --> 00:01:21.027
No plant changed its own inherited trait.

19
00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:27.447
The population changed because selected plants contributed more offspring to the

20
00:01:27.447 --> 00:01:28.447
next generation.

21
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:34.037
Repeat the cycle: inherited variation, chosen breeders, then offspring.

22
00:01:35.397 --> 00:01:38.817
Across generations, the wanted trait becomes more common.

23
00:01:40.037 --> 00:01:43.137
The trait shifts without any organism choosing to change.

24
00:01:44.317 --> 00:01:48.877
This is artificial selection, when people choose which plants or animals breed.

25
00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:51.627
Bring back the moth filter.

26
00:01:52.887 --> 00:01:57.807
In natural selection, conditions such as predators affect which inherited traits

27
00:01:57.807 --> 00:01:59.647
help organisms reproduce.

28
00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:03.947
In artificial selection, people choose the breeders.

29
00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:08.167
Both can shift inherited traits across generations.

30
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:12.467
Neither process changes an organism during life.

31
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:18.720
Both processes can shift a population in a few generations, while big changes

32
00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:19.880
usually take many.

33
00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:26.580
Inherited variation must be present, or first appear by chance, before selection can

34
00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:27.580
sort it.

35
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:31.193
Natural selection also produces adaptations.

36
00:02:32.713 --> 00:02:38.513
An adaptation is a structure, function or behaviour shaped by natural selection that

37
00:02:38.513 --> 00:02:40.033
helps survival and reproduction.

38
00:02:41.613 --> 00:02:44.893
Its value depends on the environment around the organism.

39
00:02:45.983 --> 00:02:51.583
Evolution is change in a population's inherited traits from one generation to the

40
00:02:51.583 --> 00:02:55.803
next. Natural selection is one cause of evolution.

41
00:02:57.203 --> 00:02:59.523
It is the cause that produces adaptations.

42
00:03:01.063 --> 00:03:04.043
Big changes usually build across many generations.

43
00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:09.913
The shared pattern is unequal reproduction followed by inherited change.

44
00:03:11.213 --> 00:03:14.873
The important difference is what causes that unequal reproduction.

45
00:03:16.293 --> 00:03:19.473
In artificial selection, the cause is a person's choice.

46
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:25.053
People shift inherited traits by choosing which organisms reproduce.
